      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1690, Tokugawa Tsunayoshi established a Confucian academy in Yushima, the seed of today’s Yushima Seidō.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Kanda,_Tokyo",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Kanda hosted Meiji Law School in 1881, which evolved into Meiji University and shaped modern legal education.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Kanda,_Tokyo",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Kanda, alongside Nihonbashi and Kyobashi, formed Shitamachi, Edo’s original downtown before Shinjuku and Shibuya rose.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kanda%2C_Tokyo",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Kanda Matsuri, one of Japan’s three major festivals, parades over 200 mikoshi through Kanda, Otemachi, Marunouchi, and Nihonbashi each odd year.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
